/ref 17592186054845 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049470 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186054846 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049470 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186054847 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049470 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186429503 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186049470 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186429504 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186049470 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186429505 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186049470 #edb/ref 1031 "Implicit Differentiation" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049470 #edb/ref 1033 57 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049470 #edb/ref 1034 1.147700024049995 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186049470 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "f7decca1-c481-59f9-84e3-bd90fac3ecc8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049739 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048608 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049739 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048894 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049739 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186188257 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049739 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186188290 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049739 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186188291 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049739 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186188292 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049739 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186429285 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186049739 #edb/ref 1031 "Further Modeling With First-Order ODEs" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049739 #edb/ref 1033 3860 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049739 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "0fe4fee1-477c-5881-86c7-1921fd5fff4e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186117747 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501510 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186117747 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501686 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186117747 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501774 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186117747 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501783 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186117747 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501792 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186117747 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186117747 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186117747 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186501792 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186117747 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e519677f-c9c3-5941-8d8f-ba3066c6d529" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119544 #edb/ref 1069 "e-21770" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119544 #edb/ref 1071 "Determine the differential equation satisfied by the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves $y^2 = 9C^2\\tan(x),$ where $C\\neq0$ is a parameter, in terms of $x$ and $y$ only." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119544 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "795c8239-e963-51fe-9829-49040b556231" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119544 #edb/ref 1074 "Orthogonal trajectories are families of curves that intersect another given family of curves at right angles. In other words, the tangent lines of curves from each family are perpendicular at every intersection point.\n\nLet's determine the slope of the family of curves given by $y^2 = 9C^2\\tan(x).$ First, we implicitly differentiate with respect to $x$ and solve for the derivative of $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}({y}^{2}) & =\\frac{d}{dx}\\,(9{C}^{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)) \\\\ 2y{y}^{′} & =9{C}^{2}{sec}^{2}⁡(x) \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\frac{9{C}^{2}{sec}^{2}⁡(x)}{2y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must eliminate $C.$ From the original family of curves equation, we have\n\n$$\ny^2 = 9C^2\\tan(x)\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\nC^2 = \\dfrac{y^2}{9\\tan(x)}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our slope equation, we get\n\n$$\ny' = \\dfrac{9\\left(\\dfrac{y^2}{9\\tan(x)}\\right)\\sec^2(x)}{2y}\n= \\dfrac{y\\sec^2(x)}{2\\tan(x)}.\n$$\n\nFor a curve to intersect this family orthogonally, its slope $y'_\\perp$ must be the negative reciprocal of the slope $y'$ above. Hence,\n\n$$\ny'_\\perp = -\\dfrac{1}{y'} = -\\dfrac{1}{\\left(\\dfrac{y\\sec^2(x)}{2\\tan(x)}\\right)}\n= -\\dfrac{2\\tan(x)}{y\\sec^2(x)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the orthogonal trajectories must satisfy\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}= -\\dfrac{2\\tan(x)}{y\\sec^2(x)}.\n$$\n\n**Note**: Since we divide by $y$ when forming the orthogonal slope, the orthogonal-trajectory ODE is only valid where $y\\neq 0$ (and where $\\tan(x)$ is defined). In particular, the original family forces $y=0$ whenever $\\tan(x)=0$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119545 #edb/ref 1069 "e-21771" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119545 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves $y = \\dfrac{C}{x^2},$ where $C\\neq0$ is a parameter." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119545 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "86abbc30-d597-5461-9adf-dec505ebf3f0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119545 #edb/ref 1074 "Orthogonal trajectories are families of curves that intersect another given family of curves at right angles. In other words, the tangent lines of curves from each family are perpendicular at every intersection point.\n\nLet's determine the slope of the family of curves given by $y = \\dfrac{C}{x^2}.$ First, we implicitly differentiate with respect to $x$ and solve for the derivative of $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}(y) & =\\frac{d}{dx}\\,(\\frac{C}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\ {y}^{′} & =-\\frac{2C}{{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must eliminate $C.$ From the original family of curves equation, we have\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{C}{x^2}\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\nC = x^2y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our slope equation, we get\n\n$$\ny' = -\\dfrac{2C}{x^3} = -\\dfrac{2x^2y}{x^3}\n= -\\dfrac{2y}{x}.\n$$\n\nFor a curve to intersect this family orthogonally, its slope $y'_\\perp$ must be the negative reciprocal of the slope $y'$ above. Hence,\n\n$$\ny'_\\perp = -\\dfrac{1}{y'} = -\\dfrac{1}{\\left(-\\dfrac{2y}{x}\\right)}\n= \\dfrac{x}{2y}.\n$$\n\nHence, the orthogonal trajectories must satisfy\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}= \\dfrac{x}{2y}.\n$$\n\nTo find the general solution, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y\\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx} & =x \\\\ \\int 2y\\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx}\\,dx & =\\int x\\,dx \\\\ \\int 2y\\,dy & =\\int x\\,dx \\\\ {y}^{2} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+K \\\\ 2{y}^{2}-{x}^{2} & =K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K$ is a constant of integration.\n\nTherefore, the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves $y = \\dfrac{C}{x^2},$ where $C$ is a parameter, are given by\n\n$$\n2y^2 - x^2 = K,\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is a parameter. Some curves from each family for specific parameter values are given in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/82/82bd1506c86aff662ba47618c0a6cdaf8ed1db3fa617be301fcb2d318373a913.png)\n\n**Note**: Since $C\\neq0,$ we have $y\\neq0$ on every curve in the family, so slopes $y'$ and $'_\\perp$ are well-defined." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119965 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048357 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119965 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049470 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119965 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049739 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119965 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120265 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119965 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120266 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119965 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186374875 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119965 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186374882 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119965 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186374889 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119965 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186374896 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119965 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186502070 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119965 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186502094 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119965 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186502145 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119965 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186502162 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119965 #edb/ref 1039 "Constructing the ODE Solved by the Orthogonal Trajectories of a One-Parameter Family" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119965 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186119544 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119965 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "cedd027b-bceb-5728-9d11-42c9a3c4c015" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119966 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048697 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119966 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120267 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119966 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120268 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119966 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186311862 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119966 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186374903 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119966 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186374910 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119966 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186374917 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119966 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186374924 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119966 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186502089 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119966 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Orthogonal Trajectories of a One-Parameter Family" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119966 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186119545 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119966 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "55abc561-2d1a-5da7-80f0-f52ea8c1bc3b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120028 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120949 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120028 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120950 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120028 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120951 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120028 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120952 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120028 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120028 #edb/ref 1045 "Orthogonal Trajectories" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120028 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186120949 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120028 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048894 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120028 #edb/ref 1050 480.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120028 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "a1714bc8-2198-59cf-b416-ebc74d4f062d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120267 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186374800 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120267 #edb/ref 1069 "q-336599" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120267 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120267 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves ${x}^{2}={y}^{2}+C,$ where $C$ and $K$ are parameters." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120267 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "56a4ae78-a8bc-54b5-b1dd-521d2b8670ed" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120267 #edb/ref 1074 "Orthogonal trajectories are families of curves that intersect another given family of curves at right angles. In other words, the tangent lines of curves from each family are perpendicular at every intersection point.\n\nLet's determine the slope of the family of curves given by ${x}^{2}={y}^{2}+C.$ First, we implicitly differentiate with respect to $x$ and solve for the derivative of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}({x}^{2}) & =\\frac{d}{dx}({y}^{2}+C) \\\\ 2x & =2y{y}^{′} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\frac{x}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the parameter $C$ has already been eliminated.\n\nFor a curve to intersect this family orthogonally, its slope ${y}_{⊥}^{′}$ must be the negative reciprocal of the slope ${y}^{′}$ above. Hence,\n\n$$\n{y}_{⊥}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{{y}^{′}}=-\\frac{1}{(\\frac{x}{y})}=-\\frac{y}{x}.\n$$\n\nHence, the orthogonal trajectories must satisfy\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{y}{x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the general solution, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx} & =-\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx}\\,dx & =\\int (-\\frac{1}{x})\\,dx \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\,dy & =-\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,dx \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|y| & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+{K}_{1} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y| & ={K}_{1} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|xy| & ={K}_{1} \\\\ xy & =K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K=\\pm {e}^{{K}_{1}}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nTherefore, the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves ${x}^{2}={y}^{2}+C,$ where $C$ is a parameter, are given by\n\n$$\nxy=K,\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is a parameter. Some curves from each family for specific parameter values are given in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/7f/7f85729bb9cc69865971fc11e1398738326bbc79235ea0e31c162ea1980e6d33.png)\n\n**Note**: For the slopes ${y}^{′}$ and ${y}_{⊥}^{′},$ we divide by $y$ and $x,$ respectively. So, the orthogonal trajectories are only defined on the region $x,y\\ne 0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120268 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186117747 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120268 #edb/ref 1069 "q-336616" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120268 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120268 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves $Cy={e}^{2x},$ where $C\\ne 0$ and $K$ are parameters." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120268 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "835838f0-dbf2-5a34-824d-954c2e06b295" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120268 #edb/ref 1074 "Orthogonal trajectories are families of curves that intersect another given family of curves at right angles. In other words, the tangent lines of curves from each family are perpendicular at every intersection point.\n\nLet's determine the slope of the family of curves given by $Cy={e}^{2x}.$ First, we implicitly differentiate with respect to $x$ and solve for the derivative of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}(Cy) & =\\frac{d}{dx}({e}^{2x}) \\\\ C{y}^{′} & =2{e}^{2x} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\frac{2{e}^{2x}}{C}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must eliminate $C.$ From the original family of curves equation, we have\n\n$$\nCy={e}^{2x}\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{y}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our slope equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{2{e}^{2x}}{(\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{y})}=2y.\n$$\n\nFor a curve to intersect this family orthogonally, its slope ${y}_{⊥}^{′}$ must be the negative reciprocal of the slope ${y}^{′}$ above. Hence,\n\n$$\n{y}_{⊥}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{{y}^{′}}=-\\frac{1}{2y}.\n$$\n\nHence, the orthogonal trajectories must satisfy\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{1}{2y}.\n$$\n\nTo find the general solution, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y\\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx} & =-1 \\\\ \\int 2y\\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx}\\,dx & =\\int (-1)\\,dx \\\\ \\int 2y\\,dy & =-\\int dx \\\\ {y}^{2} & =-x+K \\\\ {y}^{2}+x & =K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K$ is a constant of integration.\n\nTherefore, the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves $Cy={e}^{2x},$ where $C$ is a parameter, are given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}+x=K,\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is a parameter. Some curves from each family for specific parameter values are given in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/e9/e941d35788f4aee837220962511ca537528b04dec9efaa7f956a4ee571e14189.png)\n\n**Note**: Since $C\\ne 0,$ we have $y\\ne 0$ on every curve in the family, so slopes ${y}^{′}$ and ${}_{⊥}^{′}$ are well-defined." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120949 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120950 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120949 #edb/ref 1061 590621 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120949 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "681bf9be-ae19-5d26-92e4-2a039459df37" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120949 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186120982 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120950 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120951 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120950 #edb/ref 1061 590622 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120950 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f80cbc77-aeb2-57d8-bd75-d598f5a893e2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120950 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186120985 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120951 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120952 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120951 #edb/ref 1061 590623 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120951 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d62944ed-cf37-5d38-bf8b-3243f20787c4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120951 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119965 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120952 #edb/ref 1061 590626 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120952 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "caf2f569-2a72-5fd7-8640-737ceaac5b93" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120952 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119966 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120982 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that a *one-parameter family* of curves in the $xy$-plane is defined by\n\n$$\nF(x,y,C) = 0,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ denotes the parameter.\n\nThe **orthogonal trajectories** of a one-parameter family of curves form another one-parameter family of curves such that every curve in one family intersects at right angles every curve in the other.\n\nFor example, circles $x^2+y^2=C^2$ (with parameter $C$) and lines through the origin $y=Kx$ (with parameter $K$) are orthogonal trajectories.\n\n![](images/ca/ca980af3c5db940a9ab95567dfaa1a69ea8afb9f4f42f772bb446df67df129b8.png)\n\nNow, let $F(x,y,C) = 0$ be a family of curves with parameter $C.$ Suppose we can\n\n- implicitly differentiate it with respect to $x,$ and\n\n- eliminate $C$ using the derived and defining equations,\n\nto get an equation in terms of $x,$ $y,$ and $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}.$  Next, assume we can solve for the derivative and obtain a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = f(x,y)\n$$\n\nthat explicitly gives the slopes of the curves satisfying this ODE (at least locally, where the slope is defined). \n\nFor a curve to intersect at right angles with every curve in the family, its slope must be the negative reciprocal of the slope of curves in the family. So, the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves must be the solutions of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = -\\dfrac{1}{f(x,y)}.\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** This is true only where $f(x,y)\\neq 0.$ If $f=0$, then original curves have slope $0$ (horizontal tangent), and orthogonal curves should have infinite slope (vertical tangent)." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120982 #edb/ref 1065 14576 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120982 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "7548fa70-5dc8-5ac1-9af7-11e3bbb227ff" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120982 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120985 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we want to determine the differential equation satisfied by the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves $xy^3 = C^2,$ where $C\\neq0$ is a parameter.\n\nOrthogonal trajectories are families of curves that intersect another given family of curves at right angles. In other words, the tangent lines of curves from each family are perpendicular at every intersection point.\n\nLet's determine the slope of the family of curves given by $xy^3 = C^2.$ First, we implicitly differentiate with respect to $x$ and solve for the derivative of $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}\\,(x{y}^{3}) & =\\frac{d}{dx}\\,({C}^{2}) \\\\ {y}^{3}+3x{y}^{2}{y}^{′} & =0 \\\\ 3x{y}^{2}{y}^{′} & =-{y}^{3} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =-\\frac{y}{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the parameter $C$ has already been eliminated.\n\nFor a curve to intersect this family orthogonally, its slope $y'_\\perp$ must be the negative reciprocal of the slope $y'$ above. Hence,\n\n$$\ny'_\\perp = -\\dfrac{1}{y'} = -\\dfrac{1}{\\left(-\\dfrac{y}{3x}\\right)}\n= \\dfrac{3x}{y}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the orthogonal trajectories must satisfy\n\n$$\n\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}= \\dfrac{3x}{y}}.\n$$\n\n**Note**: Since $C \\neq 0,$ the given family has $x\\neq0$ and $y\\neq0,$ so both $y'$ and $y'_\\perp$ are well-defined." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120985 #edb/ref 1065 14586 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120985 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "11c79020-120a-5fe5-ad88-7fc3bd154c95" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120985 #edb/ref 1067 "A Worked Example" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374800 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374801 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374800 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374802 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374800 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374803 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374800 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374804 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374800 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374805 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374800 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374800 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374800 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186374805 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374800 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "9b37d6fc-fa8e-5feb-aa49-91d50d51b274" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374801 #edb/ref 1080 "{x}^{2}y=K" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374801 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374801 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "091fb33a-0ae9-5b6f-b944-c5047129533a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374802 #edb/ref 1080 "x=Ky" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374802 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374802 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d027cb04-90fe-522b-b6f5-3473a3cb33c1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374803 #edb/ref 1080 "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=K" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374803 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374803 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "baabcc94-39c3-5d6f-a95c-51afa2f628c8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374804 #edb/ref 1080 "y=Kx" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374804 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374804 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ed9b7c20-41c6-5fa4-a52e-08948672a8c8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374805 #edb/ref 1080 "xy=K" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374805 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374805 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8899f6cb-db9f-5a42-9d08-928f71dcefaf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374889 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186374890 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374889 #edb/ref 1069 "q-337322" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374889 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374889 #edb/ref 1071 "Determine the differential equation satisfied by the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves ${y}^{2}=-\\frac{4{C}^{2}}{x},$ where $C\\ne 0$ is a parameter, in terms of $x$ and $y$ only." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374889 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6d9a7bf7-c360-5b6d-8323-d7ffd06fced2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374889 #edb/ref 1074 "Orthogonal trajectories are families of curves that intersect another given family of curves at right angles. In other words, the tangent lines of curves from each family are perpendicular at every intersection point.\n\nLet's determine the slope of the family of curves given by ${y}^{2}=-\\frac{4{C}^{2}}{x}.$ First, we implicitly differentiate with respect to $x$ and solve for the derivative of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}({y}^{2}) & =\\frac{d}{dx}\\,(-\\frac{4{C}^{2}}{x}) \\\\ 2y{y}^{′} & =\\frac{4{C}^{2}}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\frac{2{C}^{2}}{{x}^{2}y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must eliminate $C.$ From the original family of curves equation, we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=-\\frac{4{C}^{2}}{x}\\,⟹\\,{C}^{2}=-\\frac{x{y}^{2}}{4}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our slope equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{2(-\\frac{x{y}^{2}}{4})}{{x}^{2}y}=-\\frac{y}{2x}.\n$$\n\nFor a curve to intersect this family orthogonally, its slope ${y}_{⊥}^{′}$ must be the negative reciprocal of the slope ${y}^{′}$ above. Hence,\n\n$$\n{y}_{⊥}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{{y}^{′}}=-\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{y}{2x})}=\\frac{2x}{y}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the orthogonal trajectories must satisfy\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{2x}{y}.\n$$\n\n**Note**: For the original family, since $C\\ne 0,$ we have $y\\ne 0$ whenever $x\\ne 0,$ hence, the slopes are well-defined. Otherwise, when $x=0,$ the curve is not defined." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374890 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374891 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374890 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374892 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374890 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374893 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374890 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374894 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374890 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374895 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374890 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374890 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374890 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186374894 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374890 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "14d574f0-74bd-5e05-b29e-d5cd37a5199b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374891 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{x}{2y}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374891 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374891 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f439cf56-cadd-5440-9e4e-4cb1193e0b79" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374892 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{2x}{y}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374892 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374892 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6cd73af6-a6e1-539d-a010-2a13d927834b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374893 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{y}{2x}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374893 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374893 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6e2caae4-d1d1-56e3-ba73-717a0a9a2e43" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374894 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{2x}{y}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374894 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374894 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2e9b6079-f9fd-51b8-b5ff-31df4c6dd3f5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374895 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y}{2x}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374895 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374895 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b2122dfe-4ca3-58cb-9f80-d44f2dc84d71" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374910 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186374911 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374910 #edb/ref 1069 "q-337569" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374910 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374910 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=C,$ where $C$ and $K$ are parameters." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374910 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "20e84823-52da-5d8d-9751-7e5bcade7fd3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374910 #edb/ref 1074 "Orthogonal trajectories are families of curves that intersect another given family of curves at right angles. In other words, the tangent lines of curves from each family are perpendicular at every intersection point.\n\nLet's determine the slope of the family of curves given by ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=C.$ First, we implicitly differentiate with respect to $x$ and solve for the derivative of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}\\,({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{d}{dx}(C) \\\\ 2x+2y{y}^{′} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =-\\frac{x}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the parameter $C$ has already been eliminated.\n\nFor a curve to intersect this family orthogonally, its slope ${y}_{⊥}^{′}$ must be the negative reciprocal of the slope ${y}^{′}$ above. Hence,\n\n$$\n{y}_{⊥}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{{y}^{′}}=-\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{x}{y})}=\\frac{y}{x}.\n$$\n\nHence, the orthogonal trajectories must satisfy\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y}{x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the general solution, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx}\\,dx & =\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,dx \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\,dy & =\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,dx \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|y| & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+{K}_{1} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|y|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x| & ={K}_{1} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| & ={K}_{1} \\\\ \\frac{y}{x} & =K \\\\ y & =Kx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K=\\pm {e}^{{K}_{1}}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nTherefore, the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=C,$ where $C$ is a parameter, are given by\n\n$$\ny=Kx,\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is a parameter. Some curves from each family for specific parameter values are given in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/ca/ca980af3c5db940a9ab95567dfaa1a69ea8afb9f4f42f772bb446df67df129b8.png)\n\n**Note**: For the slopes ${y}^{′}$ and ${y}_{⊥}^{′},$ we divide by $y$ and $x,$ respectively. So, the orthogonal trajectories are only defined on the region $x,y\\ne 0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374911 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374912 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374911 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374913 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374911 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374914 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374911 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374915 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374911 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374916 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374911 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374911 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374911 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186374915 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374911 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ba512409-31cf-5e9d-99b6-4d1f84e3bf75" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374912 #edb/ref 1080 "{x}^{2}y=K" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374912 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374912 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "833addb7-0164-5f7c-bb34-29651996fbc7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374913 #edb/ref 1080 "x=Ky" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374913 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374913 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "bb7ee723-e1fc-538d-a9fe-fe42e7426afa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374914 #edb/ref 1080 "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=K" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374914 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374914 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "75811765-47db-5662-b560-125a09e37856" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374915 #edb/ref 1080 "y=Kx" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374915 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374915 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e791fc1a-5891-5df4-bee0-fa25c7da72b9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374916 #edb/ref 1080 "xy=K" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374916 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374916 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1faceebb-b201-5935-b797-b0dcbc04a0d7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374924 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186374925 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374924 #edb/ref 1069 "q-337598" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374924 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374924 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves ${y}^{2}=Cx,$ where $C\\ne 0$ and $K$ are parameters." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374924 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1112c629-7ea8-5085-813a-086cecee9fd0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374924 #edb/ref 1074 "Orthogonal trajectories are families of curves that intersect another given family of curves at right angles. In other words, the tangent lines of curves from each family are perpendicular at every intersection point.\n\nLet's determine the slope of the family of curves given by ${y}^{2}=Cx.$ First, we implicitly differentiate with respect to $x$ and solve for the derivative of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}({y}^{2}) & =\\frac{d}{dx}(Cx) \\\\ 2y{y}^{′} & =C \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\frac{C}{2y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must eliminate $C.$ From the original family of curves equation, we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=Cx\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our slope equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{(\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x})}{2y}=\\frac{y}{2x}.\n$$\n\nFor a curve to intersect this family orthogonally, its slope ${y}_{⊥}^{′}$ must be the negative reciprocal of the slope ${y}^{′}$ above. Hence,\n\n$$\n{y}_{⊥}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{{y}^{′}}=-\\frac{1}{(\\frac{y}{2x})}=-\\frac{2x}{y}.\n$$\n\nHence, the orthogonal trajectories must satisfy\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{2x}{y}.\n$$\n\nTo find the general solution, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx} & =-2x \\\\ \\int y\\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx}\\,dx & =\\int (-2x)\\,dx \\\\ \\int y\\,dy & =-2\\int x\\,dx \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{2} & =-{x}^{2}+K \\\\ {y}^{2}+2{x}^{2} & =K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K$ is a constant of integration.\n\nTherefore, the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves ${y}^{2}=Cx,$ where $C$ is a parameter, are given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}+2{x}^{2}=K,\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is a parameter. Some curves from each family for specific parameter values are given in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/03/039a42b728cad07b3e8f5dfd2f1607b36819e586f40e54e06d942679bb4ededa.png)\n\n**Note**: Since $C\\ne 0,$ we have $y\\ne 0$ on every curve in the family (except possibly at $x=0$), so slopes ${y}^{′}$ and ${}_{⊥}^{′}$ are well-defined away from $y=0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374925 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374926 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374925 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374927 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374925 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374928 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374925 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374929 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374925 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374930 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374925 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374925 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374925 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186374930 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374925 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "080d2f6c-6aee-5b11-b159-6789b41fc162" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374926 #edb/ref 1080 "{y}^{2}-2{x}^{2}=K" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374926 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374926 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6f607760-f38e-5bf2-a1b4-0d34c34ea859" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374927 #edb/ref 1080 "{y}^{2}+Cx=K" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374927 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [1