17592186502059 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501669 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502059 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501850 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502059 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501911 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502059 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502059 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502059 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186501669 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502059 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f427581a-7c85-5678-8de9-9e496c491b50" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502070 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502008 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502070 #edb/ref 1069 "q-337311" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502070 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502070 #edb/ref 1071 "Determine the differential equation satisfied by the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves ${x}^{2}y=\\frac{1}{C},$ where $C\\ne 0$ is a parameter." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502070 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0166ce2f-2829-5697-886e-176606ebec4c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502070 #edb/ref 1074 "Orthogonal trajectories are families of curves that intersect another given family of curves at right angles. In other words, the tangent lines of curves from each family are perpendicular at every intersection point.\n\nLet's determine the slope of the family of curves given by ${x}^{2}y=\\frac{1}{C}.$ First, we implicitly differentiate with respect to $x$ and solve for the derivative of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}\\,({x}^{2}y) & =\\frac{d}{dx}\\,(\\frac{1}{C}) \\\\ 2xy+{x}^{2}{y}^{′} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}{y}^{′} & =-2xy \\\\ {y}^{′} & =-\\frac{2y}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the parameter $C$ has already been eliminated.\n\nFor a curve to intersect this family orthogonally, its slope ${y}_{⊥}^{′}$ must be the negative reciprocal of the slope ${y}^{′}$ above. Hence,\n\n$$\n{y}_{⊥}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{{y}^{′}}=-\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{2y}{x})}=\\frac{x}{2y}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the orthogonal trajectories must satisfy\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{x}{2y}.\n$$\n\n**Note**: Since $C\\ne 0,$ the given family has $x\\ne 0$ and $y\\ne 0,$ so both ${y}^{′}$ and ${y}_{⊥}^{′}$ are well-defined." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502089 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502033 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502089 #edb/ref 1069 "q-337573" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502089 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502089 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves $\\frac{x}{y}={C}^{2},$ where $C$ and $K$ are parameters." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502089 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "33804b3c-aea9-58d0-9654-8a84f729c14e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502089 #edb/ref 1074 "Orthogonal trajectories are families of curves that intersect another given family of curves at right angles. In other words, the tangent lines of curves from each family are perpendicular at every intersection point.\n\nLet's determine the slope of the family of curves given by $\\frac{x}{y}={C}^{2}.$ First, we implicitly differentiate with respect to $x$ and solve for the derivative of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}\\,(\\frac{x}{y}) & =\\frac{d}{dx}\\,({C}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{y-x{y}^{′}}{{y}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ y-x{y}^{′} & =0 \\\\ x{y}^{′} & =y \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\frac{y}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the parameter $C$ has already been eliminated.\n\nFor a curve to intersect this family orthogonally, its slope ${y}_{⊥}^{′}$ must be the negative reciprocal of the slope ${y}^{′}$ above. Hence,\n\n$$\n{y}_{⊥}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{{y}^{′}}=-\\frac{1}{(\\frac{y}{x})}=-\\frac{x}{y}.\n$$\n\nHence, the orthogonal trajectories must satisfy\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{x}{y}.\n$$\n\nTo find the general solution, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx} & =-x \\\\ \\int y\\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx}\\,dx & =\\int (-x)\\,dx \\\\ \\int y\\,dy & =-\\int x\\,dx \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{2} & =-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+{K}_{1} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K=2{K}_{1}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nTherefore, the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves $\\frac{x}{y}={C}^{2},$ where $C$ is a parameter, are given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=K,\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is a parameter. Some curves from each family for specific parameter values are given in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/a3/a3854e6c47f28ce601ef2395379bea788e954c4e4057331772b5840cd7073237.png)\n\n**Note**: For the slopes ${y}^{′}$ and ${y}_{⊥}^{′},$ we divide by $x$ and $y,$ respectively. So, the orthogonal trajectories are only defined on the region $x,y\\ne 0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502094 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502059 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502094 #edb/ref 1069 "q-337317" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502094 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502094 #edb/ref 1071 "Determine the differential equation satisfied by the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves $y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(C),$ where $C\\ne 0$ is a parameter." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502094 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3933c79b-01f9-56d3-9429-e1ebc3d9a1ea" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502094 #edb/ref 1074 "Orthogonal trajectories are families of curves that intersect another given family of curves at right angles. In other words, the tangent lines of curves from each family are perpendicular at every intersection point.\n\nLet's determine the slope of the family of curves given by $y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(C).$ First, we implicitly differentiate with respect to $x$ and solve for the derivative of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}\\,(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)) & =\\frac{d}{dx}\\,(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(C)) \\\\ {y}^{′}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+\\frac{y}{x} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x) & =-\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =-\\frac{y}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the parameter $C$ has already been eliminated.\n\nFor a curve to intersect this family orthogonally, its slope ${y}_{⊥}^{′}$ must be the negative reciprocal of the slope ${y}^{′}$ above. Hence,\n\n$$\n{y}_{⊥}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{{y}^{′}}=-\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{y}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)})}=\\frac{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{y}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the orthogonal trajectories must satisfy\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{y}.\n$$\n\n**Note**: Since $C\\ne 0,$ the given family has $x>0,$ $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\ne 0,$ and $y\\ne 0,$ so both ${y}^{′}$ and ${y}_{⊥}^{′}$ are well-defined." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502145 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502006 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502145 #edb/ref 1069 "q-337315" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502145 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502145 #edb/ref 1071 "Determine the differential equation satisfied by the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves $y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)=C,$ where $C\\ne 0$ is a parameter." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502145 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cc13bee1-bf2a-51a5-a80d-77b695b1c60b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502145 #edb/ref 1074 "Orthogonal trajectories are families of curves that intersect another given family of curves at right angles. In other words, the tangent lines of curves from each family are perpendicular at every intersection point.\n\nLet's determine the slope of the family of curves given by $y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)=C.$ First, we implicitly differentiate with respect to $x$ and solve for the derivative of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}\\,(y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)) & =\\frac{d}{dx}\\,(C) \\\\ {y}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) & =-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) \\\\ {y}^{′} & =-\\frac{y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the parameter $C$ has already been eliminated.\n\nFor a curve to intersect this family orthogonally, its slope ${y}_{⊥}^{′}$ must be the negative reciprocal of the slope ${y}^{′}$ above. Hence,\n\n$$\n{y}_{⊥}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{{y}^{′}}=-\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)})}=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the orthogonal trajectories must satisfy\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}.\n$$\n\n**Note**: Since $C\\ne 0,$ the given family has $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\ne 0$ and $y\\ne 0,$ so both ${y}^{′}$ and ${y}_{⊥}^{′}$ are well-defined." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502162 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502047 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502162 #edb/ref 1069 "q-337313" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502162 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502162 #edb/ref 1071 "Determine the differential equation satisfied by the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves $\\frac{x}{y}={C}^{2},$ where $C\\ne 0$ is a parameter." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502162 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f66e36cd-9bf6-56e0-b87a-016d297333d7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502162 #edb/ref 1074 "Orthogonal trajectories are families of curves that intersect another given family of curves at right angles. In other words, the tangent lines of curves from each family are perpendicular at every intersection point.\n\nLet's determine the slope of the family of curves given by $\\frac{x}{y}={C}^{2}.$ First, we implicitly differentiate with respect to $x$ and solve for the derivative of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}\\,(\\frac{x}{y}) & =\\frac{d}{dx}\\,({C}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{y-x{y}^{′}}{{y}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ y-x{y}^{′} & =0 \\\\ x{y}^{′} & =y \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\frac{y}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the parameter $C$ has already been eliminated.\n\nFor a curve to intersect this family orthogonally, its slope ${y}_{⊥}^{′}$ must be the negative reciprocal of the slope ${y}^{′}$ above. Hence,\n\n$$\n{y}_{⊥}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{{y}^{′}}=-\\frac{1}{(\\frac{y}{x})}=-\\frac{x}{y}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the orthogonal trajectories must satisfy\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{x}{y}.\n$$\n\n**Note**: Since $C\\ne 0,$ the given family has $x\\ne 0$ and $y\\ne 0,$ so both ${y}^{′}$ and ${y}_{⊥}^{′}$ are well-defined." #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
